1.Download a sample Terms & Conditions document from any popular app (like Zomato, Flipkart, or Instagram), and perform a static review to identify at least 5 unclear or ambiguous statements that could confuse users.
Answer :
1.] Acceptance by simply using the service
>Statement: Zomato says that actually using the platform can be treated as acceptance of the Terms.
>Why it is unclear: A user may not realize that simply browsing or using the app means they have legally accepted the Terms.
>Suggested improvement: Clearly state which actions count as acceptance and when acceptance takes effect.
2.] Changes to the Terms
>Statement: Zomato can change or update the Terms at its “entirely at its own discretion.”
>Why it is ambiguous: It does not clearly explain how users will be notified about important changes.
>Suggested improvement: Specify how users will be informed and when major changes become effective.
3.] “Objectionable” content
>Statement: Users must not post content that is “otherwise objectionable.”
>Why it is unclear: “Objectionable” is subjective and different users may interpret it differently.
>Suggested improvement: Give clear examples or measurable criteria for content considered objectionable.
4.] “Standards of good taste”
>Statement: Users must not post material that violates the “standards of good taste or the standards of the Services.”
>Why it is ambiguous: There is no clear definition of what “good taste” means.
>Suggested improvement: Replace this phrase with specific content rules and examples.
5.] Changes to content/features without notice
>Statement: Zomato states that it may make changes and corrections to content at any time without prior notice.
>Why it may confuse users: Users may not know which types of information or features can change without notification.
>Suggested improvement: Specify whether this applies to prices, menus, availability, features, or other information.


2.Create a comparison table listing 3 key differences between static testing and dynamic testing, using real-life examples from apps you use daily (such as a privacy policy review vs. actually filling a signup form).
Answer :
Static Testing vs Dynamic Testing
1.)Meaning
Static Testing: Testing is done without running the application.
Dynamic Testing: Testing is done by running and using the application.
2.)What is checked
Static Testing: Requirements, documents, designs, and code are reviewed to find errors.
Dynamic Testing: The actual functionality and behavior of the application are tested.
3.)Real-life example
Static Testing: Reviewing a privacy policy or signup requirements to find unclear or missing information.
Dynamic Testing: Filling out a signup form with valid and invalid details to check whether registration works correctly.


3.Imagine you are part of a code walkthrough for a new feature in a food delivery app (like Swiggy) — write 5 questions or points you would raise while reviewing the code for the 'Apply Coupon' function.
Answer :

During a code walkthrough for the “Apply Coupon” function in a Swiggy-like food delivery app, I would raise these 5 points:
1)Coupon Validation: Does the code check whether the coupon is valid, expired, or already used?
2)Minimum Order Value: Does it correctly verify conditions such as a minimum order amount before applying the coupon?
3)Discount Calculation: Is the discount calculated correctly, including maximum discount limits and percentage/fixed discounts?
4)Error Handling: What happens when the user enters an invalid coupon? Is a clear error message displayed without changing the order total?
5)Security & Duplicate Use: Does the system prevent users from manipulating the coupon request or applying the same coupon multiple times?


4.Conduct a mock inspection of a sample bug report from any open-source project (find one on GitHub or use a template online), and list 3 improvements you would suggest to make the report clearer and more useful for developers.Hint: Look for missing steps, unclear expected results, or vague descriptions.
Answer :

Mock Inspection of a Sample Bug Report:-
For the mock inspection, I referred to an open-source GitHub issue format and compared it with recommended bug-report practices. A good bug report should clearly include reproduction steps, expected behavior, actual behavior, and environment details.

-Sample Bug Description:
“Login is not working. The app shows an error when I try to log in.”
-Sample Bug Description:
“Login is not working. The app shows an error when I try to log in.”

3 Improvements
1.)Add Clear Steps to Reproduce
The report should specify exact steps, for example:
“Open the app → Enter valid email and password → Click Login → Observe the error.”
This helps developers reproduce the issue consistently.
2.)Clearly Mention Expected and Actual Results
Instead of only saying “login is not working,” the report should state:
Expected: User should be logged in and taken to the home page.
Actual: An “Invalid request” error is displayed.
This makes the defect much easier to understand.
3.)Include Environment Details
Add information such as app version, device, operating system, and browser version. This helps developers determine whether the issue is specific to a particular environment.
